14
00:00:53.942 --> 00:00:56.672
The solved exam 1 that
your classmates shared

15
00:00:56.712 --> 00:00:59.772
has ten short problems,
roughly one per topic.

16
00:00:59.822 --> 00:01:03.929
They were: a noise-figure cascade,
the local oscillator of a

17
00:01:03.969 --> 00:01:08.282
superheterodyne, the ratio between
day and night maximum usable

18
00:01:08.322 --> 00:01:12.705
frequencies, the input impedance
of a line, a VSWR, a coaxial

19
00:01:12.745 --> 00:01:17.127
cable from a datasheet, a cascade
intercept point, a return-loss

20
00:01:17.167 --> 00:01:23.253
and cable-loss check, an FM bandwidth, and
a compression and noise-floor question.

21
00:01:23.303 --> 00:01:26.483
None of them needs long
algebra: the skill is choosing

22
00:01:26.523 --> 00:01:29.346
the right formula and
converting quickly between

23
00:01:29.386 --> 00:01:33.672
decibels and linear units, which
is exactly what we will practise.

24
00:01:35.322 --> 00:01:38.674
The electromagnetic spectrum
used for radio is divided

25
00:01:38.714 --> 00:01:42.580
by the I T U into bands, each
one a decade of frequency.

26
00:01:42.630 --> 00:01:48.607
Low frequency goes from 30 to 300
kHz, medium frequency up to 3 MHz,

27
00:01:48.647 --> 00:01:54.070
high frequency up to 30 MHz, very
high frequency up to 300 MHz,

28
00:01:54.110 --> 00:02:00.047
ultra high frequency up to 3 GHz and
super high frequency up to 30 GHz.

29
00:02:00.097 --> 00:02:04.278
Aviation uses almost all of them:
non-directional beacons sit between

30
00:02:04.318 --> 00:02:11.681
190 and 535 kHz, long-range voice
over the oceans uses HF, between 2

31
00:02:11.721 --> 00:02:20.207
and 30 MHz, and the familiar VHF
voice band runs from 118 to 137 MHz.

32
00:02:20.257 --> 00:02:25.830
Just below it, from 108 to
118 MHz, are the VOR and

33
00:02:25.870 --> 00:02:30.917
the ILS localizer, with the
glide slope near 330 MHz.

34
00:02:30.967 --> 00:02:35.117
Around 1 GHz, the L band holds
the DME, the secondary radar at

35
00:02:35.157 --> 00:02:43.904
1030 and 1090 MHz and GPS at
1575 MHz, and higher up come the

36
00:02:43.944 --> 00:02:50.055
radio altimeter at 4.3 GHz and
the weather radar near 9.4 GHz.

37
00:02:51.705 --> 00:02:56.107
The first formula of the course links
wavelength, frequency and the medium.

38
00:02:56.157 --> 00:03:00.329
The wavelength is the speed of light
divided by the frequency and by the

39
00:03:00.369 --> 00:03:05.575
square root of the effective permittivity,
because waves slow down in a dielectric.

40
00:03:05.625 --> 00:03:08.384
In air the permittivity is one, so 3 times

41
00:03:08.424 --> 00:03:13.277
ten to the 8 over ten
to the 8 gives 3.00 m.

42
00:03:13.327 --> 00:03:17.472
In a polyethylene cable, with a
permittivity of 2.3, the square

43
00:03:17.512 --> 00:03:23.274
root is 1.517, and the wavelength
shrinks to 1.98 m: this is why

44
00:03:23.314 --> 00:03:27.458
a quarter-wave cable section is
shorter than a quarter-wave antenna.

45
00:03:27.508 --> 00:03:34.325
And in water, with a permittivity of
80.4, the wavelength is only 0.335 m.

46
00:03:35.975 --> 00:03:39.913
Radio waves travel through an
atmosphere with distinct layers.

47
00:03:39.963 --> 00:03:44.061
The troposphere, from the
ground to about 10 to 12 km, is

48
00:03:44.101 --> 00:03:47.372
where the weather is, and it
bends VHF waves slightly,

49
00:03:47.412 --> 00:03:51.191
extending their range a little
beyond the optical horizon.

50
00:03:51.241 --> 00:03:56.844
Above the stratosphere, from
50 to 80 km up to 400 or 500

51
00:03:56.884 --> 00:04:00.354
km, the ionosphere is ionised
by solar radiation, so its

52
00:04:00.394 --> 00:04:04.757
properties change between day and
night and with solar activity.

53
00:04:04.807 --> 00:04:08.308
The D layer, present only in
daylight, absorbs the lower

54
00:04:08.348 --> 00:04:12.482
frequencies below about 4
MHz, while the E, F1 and F2

55
00:04:12.522 --> 00:04:17.742
layers reflect waves up to about
5, 10 and 30 MHz respectively.

56
00:04:17.792 --> 00:04:20.557
At night the D layer disappears
and the other layers

57
00:04:20.597 --> 00:04:24.075
weaken, which is why the usable
frequencies drop at night.

58
00:04:25.725 --> 00:04:30.161
There are four ways for a radio wave to
get from the transmitter to the receiver.

59
00:04:30.211 --> 00:04:33.679
The ground or surface wave follows
the curvature of the Earth,

60
00:04:33.719 --> 00:04:38.260
from about 20 kHz up to 50 MHz;
its losses grow with frequency

61
00:04:38.300 --> 00:04:41.711
and are larger over land than
over sea, and they are smallest

62
00:04:41.751 --> 00:04:46.061
with vertical polarisation, which is
why beacon antennas are vertical.

63
00:04:46.111 --> 00:04:51.511
Above about 50 MHz the ground wave dies
quickly and communication relies on the

64
00:04:51.551 --> 00:04:56.724
space wave: a direct, line-of-sight ray,
plus a ray reflected from the ground.

65
00:04:56.774 --> 00:05:00.024
In the HF band, the ionosphere
can reflect the sky

66
00:05:00.064 --> 00:05:02.928
wave back to Earth, so
a single hop can cover

67
00:05:02.968 --> 00:05:07.318
thousands of kilometres, which
is how oceanic HF voice works.

68
00:05:07.368 --> 00:05:11.002
Finally, tropospheric waves
are scattered or ducted in the

69
00:05:11.042 --> 00:05:15.763
lower atmosphere, occasionally
carrying VHF far beyond the horizon.

70
00:05:17.413 --> 00:05:21.478
This is a ground-wave problem
with an empirical range formula.

71
00:05:21.528 --> 00:05:25.338
The square root of 5 000 watts is 70.7.

72
00:05:25.388 --> 00:05:30.475
Over sea the range is
3 times that, 212 nmi.

73
00:05:30.525 --> 00:05:35.658
Over land it is only 2
times that, 141 nmi.

74
00:05:35.708 --> 00:05:40.115
So the difference is 70.7 nmi,
a third of the sea range,

75
00:05:40.155 --> 00:05:44.301
because the ground wave loses more
energy over poorly conducting land.

76
00:05:45.951 --> 00:05:49.982
Above 50 MHz, the range is
set by the radio horizon.

77
00:05:50.032 --> 00:05:54.291
VHF voice, VOR and DME all
need line of sight between the

78
00:05:54.331 --> 00:05:58.776
antennas, so the curvature of
the Earth limits the distance.

79
00:05:58.826 --> 00:06:03.323
Binns gives the practical formula:
the range in nautical miles is 1.23

80
00:06:03.363 --> 00:06:07.396
times the sum of the square roots
of the two antenna heights in feet.

81
00:06:07.446 --> 00:06:10.021
Because of the square
roots, the range grows

82
00:06:10.061 --> 00:06:13.740
slowly: flying four times
higher only doubles the range.

83
00:06:13.790 --> 00:06:17.388
The trap is in the units: the
heights must be in feet and the

84
00:06:17.428 --> 00:06:23.683
result is in nautical miles, so convert
metres first, dividing by 0.3048.

85
00:06:25.333 --> 00:06:27.286
Two quick applications.

86
00:06:27.336 --> 00:06:30.304
For part a, the ground
antenna is 10 m high,

87
00:06:30.344 --> 00:06:35.569
that is 32.8 ft, whose
square root is 5.73.

88
00:06:35.619 --> 00:06:41.358
Then the square root of the aircraft
height is 125 over 1.23 minus

89
00:06:41.398 --> 00:06:50.005
5.73, which is 95.9, so the aircraft
must fly at about 9 200 ft, 2.8 km.

90
00:06:50.055 --> 00:06:53.394
For part b the receiver is
at ground level, so only the

91
00:06:53.434 --> 00:06:57.264
aircraft height counts: convert
each altitude to feet, take the

92
00:06:57.304 --> 00:07:04.105
square root, multiply by 1.23, and
multiply by 1.852 to get kilometres.

93
00:07:04.155 --> 00:07:13.712
At 150 m the range is 27 nmi, about 50
km, and at 300 m it is about 72 km.

94
00:07:13.762 --> 00:07:24.790
At 1 500 m it reaches 160 km, at
3 000 m 226 km and at 5 000 m 292

95
00:07:24.830 --> 00:07:29.205
km: the plot is a square-root curve,
steep at first and then flattening.

96
00:07:30.855 --> 00:07:36.371
For sky waves, the key quantity is the
electron density of the ionosphere.

97
00:07:36.421 --> 00:07:40.704
A wave sent straight up is reflected
only if its frequency is below the

98
00:07:40.744 --> 00:07:45.270
critical frequency, fc, equal to 9
times the square root of the electron

99
00:07:45.310 --> 00:07:49.983
density, with fc in hertz and the
density in electrons per cubic metre.

100
00:07:50.033 --> 00:07:54.039
Last year's solution used the same
formula in friendlier units: fc in

101
00:07:54.079 --> 00:07:58.437
megahertz equals 9 times ten to the
minus 3 times the square root of

102
00:07:58.477 --> 00:08:03.108
the density in electrons per cubic
centimetre; both give the same number.

103
00:08:03.158 --> 00:08:06.266
A wave that hits the layer
obliquely, leaving the

104
00:08:06.306 --> 00:08:08.965
ground at an elevation angle theta, can be

105
00:08:09.005 --> 00:08:11.985
reflected up to a higher
frequency, the maximum

106
00:08:12.025 --> 00:08:16.488
usable frequency, approximately
fc divided by the sine of theta.

107
00:08:16.538 --> 00:08:21.142
Below the MUF there is also a lowest
usable frequency, the LUF, set by

108
00:08:21.182 --> 00:08:25.862
absorption in the D layer, so the
usable window lies between the two.

109
00:08:25.912 --> 00:08:30.328
The MUF by day is roughly twice the
MUF by night, and in practice one

110
00:08:30.368 --> 00:08:36.456
works at the optimum working frequency,
85 % of the MUF, for a safety margin.

111
00:08:38.106 --> 00:08:41.683
Both sky-wave problems use
the same two formulas.

112
00:08:41.733 --> 00:08:45.354
Convert the density first: 5
times ten to the 5 per cubic

113
00:08:45.394 --> 00:08:48.957
centimetre is 5 times ten to
the 11 per cubic metre, so

114
00:08:48.997 --> 00:08:54.227
the critical frequency is 9
times its square root, 6.36 MHz.

115
00:08:54.277 --> 00:08:59.626
In part a, the sine of the
angle is 6.36 over 24.58,

116
00:08:59.666 --> 00:09:05.285
which is 0.259, so the elevation
angle is 15.0 degrees.

117
00:09:05.335 --> 00:09:08.870
In part b, assume a single hop
with the reflection midway, and

118
00:09:08.910 --> 00:09:15.269
a flat Earth: half of 1 300
statute miles is 1 046 km, so the

119
00:09:15.309 --> 00:09:22.578
tangent of the elevation is 350
over 1 046, giving 18.5 degrees.

120
00:09:22.628 --> 00:09:29.181
Then the MUF is 6.36 over the
sine of 18.5 degrees, 20.1 MHz,

121
00:09:29.221 --> 00:09:35.273
and the optimum working frequency
is 85 % of that, 17.1 MHz.

122
00:09:35.323 --> 00:09:39.940
If the miles are read as nautical
miles, the angle is 16.2 degrees

123
00:09:39.980 --> 00:09:45.576
and the OWF 19.4 MHz, so always
state your assumption on the exam.

124
00:09:47.226 --> 00:09:50.067
This came up in last year's
exam, with the densities

125
00:09:50.107 --> 00:09:53.393
read from a graph of electron
density against height.

126
00:09:53.443 --> 00:09:55.500
Use the critical-frequency formula in

127
00:09:55.540 --> 00:09:58.611
megahertz with the density
per cubic centimetre.

128
00:09:58.661 --> 00:10:02.685
By day, 9 times ten to the minus
3 times the square root of ten to

129
00:10:02.725 --> 00:10:09.866
the 5 is 2.85 MHz; by night, with 2
times ten to the 4, it is 1.27 MHz.

130
00:10:09.916 --> 00:10:12.204
Dividing by the sine of 20 degrees, the

131
00:10:12.244 --> 00:10:17.627
MUF is 8.32 MHz by day
and 3.72 MHz by night.

132
00:10:17.677 --> 00:10:22.296
The ratio is the square root of the
density ratio, the square root of 5,

133
00:10:22.336 --> 00:10:27.950
which is 2.24, consistent with the rule
that the day MUF is about twice the

134
00:10:27.990 --> 00:10:33.248
night MUF; notice that the angle cancels,
so the ratio needs no trigonometry.

135
00:10:34.898 --> 00:10:38.753
Obstacles and discontinuities modify
propagation in four ways, and

136
00:10:38.793 --> 00:10:43.038
the size of the obstacle relative
to the wavelength decides which.

137
00:10:43.088 --> 00:10:46.246
Reflection happens when a
wave meets a flat object much

138
00:10:46.286 --> 00:10:50.067
larger than the wavelength,
such as the ground or a hangar.

139
00:10:50.117 --> 00:10:53.356
Refraction happens when a wave
passes from one medium into

140
00:10:53.396 --> 00:10:57.816
another and changes direction, as in
the troposphere and the ionosphere.

141
00:10:57.866 --> 00:11:00.632
Diffraction happens when
a wave meets an edge and

142
00:11:00.672 --> 00:11:03.209
bends around it, following
the profile of the

143
00:11:03.249 --> 00:11:06.692
discontinuity, which gives
some coverage behind hills.

144
00:11:06.742 --> 00:11:10.738
Scattering happens when the obstacles
are smaller than the wavelength, like

145
00:11:10.778 --> 00:11:15.648
raindrops for radar waves, which fragment
and re-radiate the wave in all directions.

146
00:11:17.298 --> 00:11:21.698
The slides finish propagation with a
graph of atmospheric absorption against

147
00:11:21.738 --> 00:11:26.762
frequency, and a question: which bands
would you choose for secure applications?

148
00:11:26.812 --> 00:11:30.306
The absorption has peaks where
water vapour and oxygen molecules

149
00:11:30.346 --> 00:11:38.041
resonate: water at 22 and 183 GHz,
and oxygen at 60 and 119 GHz.

150
00:11:38.091 --> 00:11:42.211
Between the peaks there are
windows, such as 35 and 94

151
00:11:42.251 --> 00:11:45.952
GHz, where millimetre-wave
links and radars can reach far.

152
00:11:46.002 --> 00:11:49.527
For a secure link you want the
opposite: work at an absorption

153
00:11:49.567 --> 00:11:53.839
peak, especially the 60 GHz oxygen
line, where the signal dies out

154
00:11:53.879 --> 00:11:58.311
within a few kilometres, so an
eavesdropper far away cannot receive it.

155
00:11:59.961 --> 00:12:03.271
Now the systems that send
and receive the waves.

156
00:12:03.321 --> 00:12:07.081
A transmitter is the system that
generates the signal, a receiver

157
00:12:07.121 --> 00:12:10.646
recovers the wanted signal from
a wide spectrum full of other

158
00:12:10.686 --> 00:12:15.420
transmitters, interference and noise,
and a transceiver is both in one box.

159
00:12:15.470 --> 00:12:18.440
The information starts as
a baseband signal, whose

160
00:12:18.480 --> 00:12:21.390
spectrum is concentrated
near zero frequency; the

161
00:12:21.430 --> 00:12:24.761
modulator converts it into a
band-pass signal around the

162
00:12:24.801 --> 00:12:29.485
carrier, which is then upconverted
and amplified by the power amplifier.

163
00:12:29.535 --> 00:12:32.936
At the other end, a low-noise
amplifier, more amplifiers,

164
00:12:32.976 --> 00:12:36.135
downconversion and a demodulator
recover the baseband

165
00:12:36.175 --> 00:12:39.455
signal with as little noise
and distortion as possible;

166
00:12:39.495 --> 00:12:43.527
we will see in part two why the
first amplifier matters most.

167
00:12:45.177 --> 00:12:49.382
Any modulated carrier can be
written as an amplitude A of t

168
00:12:49.422 --> 00:12:53.124
times the cosine of omega
C t plus a phase phi of t:

169
00:12:53.164 --> 00:12:58.623
amplitude modulation varies A, frequency
and phase modulation vary phi.

170
00:12:58.673 --> 00:13:03.369
In AM the envelope is the carrier
amplitude times one plus m times the

171
00:13:03.409 --> 00:13:08.105
message, where the modulation index
m is the message amplitude over the

172
00:13:08.145 --> 00:13:13.698
carrier amplitude, between zero and one;
watch the envelope deepen as m grows.

173
00:13:13.748 --> 00:13:17.319
In the spectrum, the carrier
is accompanied by a lower and

174
00:13:17.359 --> 00:13:20.494
an upper sideband, mirror
images of the message, so

175
00:13:20.534 --> 00:13:24.930
double-sideband AM occupies twice
the highest message frequency.

176
00:13:24.980 --> 00:13:30.011
In the slide example, an audio
signal from 20 Hz to 30 kHz on a 100

177
00:13:30.051 --> 00:13:35.743
kHz carrier needs 60 kHz, and each
sideband is 29.98 kHz wide: the

178
00:13:35.783 --> 00:13:44.730
lower one runs from 70 to 99.98 kHz and
the upper one from 100.02 to 130 kHz.

179
00:13:46.380 --> 00:13:48.813
In frequency modulation
the amplitude stays

180
00:13:48.853 --> 00:13:51.401
constant and the phase
is the integral of the

181
00:13:51.441 --> 00:13:55.241
message, so the instantaneous
frequency follows the message.

182
00:13:55.291 --> 00:13:59.372
The peak frequency excursion is
the deviation, delta f, and the

183
00:13:59.412 --> 00:14:04.604
modulation index m f is the deviation
divided by the modulating frequency.

184
00:14:04.654 --> 00:14:08.830
The bandwidth that contains
about 98 % of the power is

185
00:14:08.870 --> 00:14:12.083
given by Carson's rule: twice
the sum of the deviation

186
00:14:12.123 --> 00:14:16.207
and the modulating frequency, or
twice f m times m f plus one.

187
00:14:16.257 --> 00:14:19.889
The spectrum is a set of lines
spaced f m apart around the

188
00:14:19.929 --> 00:14:23.497
carrier, with amplitudes given
by Bessel functions of the

189
00:14:23.537 --> 00:14:27.938
index, and Carson's band, shaded,
contains the significant ones.

190
00:14:29.588 --> 00:14:31.575
Two quick FM problems.

191
00:14:31.625 --> 00:14:34.545
In part a the deviation
is the index times the

192
00:14:34.585 --> 00:14:38.988
modulating frequency, 5
times 10, which is 50 kHz.

193
00:14:39.038 --> 00:14:46.153
Carson's rule gives twice 50 plus 10,
that is 120 kHz, centred on 250 MHz,

194
00:14:46.193 --> 00:14:51.613
with spectral lines every 10 kHz whose
heights follow the Bessel functions of 5.

195
00:14:51.663 --> 00:14:54.840
In last year's exam the
index was one, so the

196
00:14:54.880 --> 00:14:59.304
deviation equals the
modulating frequency, 10 kHz.

197
00:14:59.354 --> 00:15:02.219
And the bandwidth is 2 times 10 times one

198
00:15:02.259 --> 00:15:06.241
plus one, 40 kHz, as both
solved copies found.

199
00:15:07.891 --> 00:15:09.570
With a digital message the three

200
00:15:09.610 --> 00:15:12.699
parameters of the carrier
give three basic keyings.

201
00:15:12.749 --> 00:15:17.638
In amplitude shift keying a one sends
the carrier and a zero sends nothing.

202
00:15:17.688 --> 00:15:22.740
In frequency shift keying a one and a zero
are sent at two different frequencies.

203
00:15:22.790 --> 00:15:28.644
In phase shift keying the phase jumps
by 180 degrees between the two symbols.

204
00:15:28.694 --> 00:15:32.673
The modulators on the slides are
simple switches or multipliers,

205
00:15:32.713 --> 00:15:36.819
and the demodulators are an envelope
detector for ASK, a pair of

206
00:15:36.859 --> 00:15:41.919
filters for FSK and a coherent
multiplication with the carrier for PSK.

207
00:15:43.569 --> 00:15:47.012
A band is the whole range of
spectrum a standard may use, a

208
00:15:47.052 --> 00:15:50.496
channel is the bandwidth of a
single user, and multiplexing

209
00:15:50.536 --> 00:15:54.310
lets several signals share one
medium without interfering.

210
00:15:54.360 --> 00:15:58.498
In frequency-division multiplexing
each user is given its own frequency

211
00:15:58.538 --> 00:16:03.301
slot, with guard bands between them so
that real filters can separate them.

212
00:16:03.351 --> 00:16:06.196
In time-division multiplexing
users take turns,

213
00:16:06.236 --> 00:16:09.715
each one using the whole
band during its time slot.

214
00:16:09.765 --> 00:16:13.428
In the slide problem each
signal carries 6 MHz of video

215
00:16:13.468 --> 00:16:17.249
and needs a 4 MHz guard band,
so each channel occupies a

216
00:16:17.289 --> 00:16:21.400
10 MHz slot, but only if each
channel is single-sideband.

217
00:16:21.450 --> 00:16:28.987
With the upper sideband, a carrier at 700
MHz puts the video from 700 to 706 MHz,

218
00:16:29.027 --> 00:16:36.854
the guard band takes it to 710, the next
carrier is at 710, and so on up to 790

219
00:16:36.894 --> 00:16:43.795
MHz; the slide's figure marks its carriers
at the slot centres, 705, 715 and so

220
00:16:43.835 --> 00:16:48.827
on, which is only schematic, although for
a hand sketch 6 MHz blocks centred on

221
00:16:48.867 --> 00:16:57.083
those carriers, 702 to 708 MHz with 4 MHz
guards, match the professor's own figure.

222
00:16:57.133 --> 00:17:00.535
With double sideband each
channel would occupy 12 MHz

223
00:17:00.575 --> 00:17:04.530
and need a spacing of 16 MHz,
so state your assumption.

224
00:17:04.580 --> 00:17:08.851
Either way the ten channels
fill 700 to 800 MHz, and the

225
00:17:08.891 --> 00:17:14.183
10 GHz upconverter moves the
multiplex to 10.7 to 10.8 GHz.

226
00:17:15.833 --> 00:17:18.328
In a direct-conversion
transmitter the carrier

227
00:17:18.368 --> 00:17:20.972
frequency equals the
local-oscillator frequency,

228
00:17:21.012 --> 00:17:24.612
and modulation and upconversion
happen in the same circuit.

229
00:17:24.662 --> 00:17:29.014
It is usually built with in-phase and
quadrature branches: the bit stream

230
00:17:29.054 --> 00:17:33.225
is divided into pairs of bits, one
modulating a cosine and the other a

231
00:17:33.265 --> 00:17:38.248
sine of the same carrier, which increases
robustness and spectral efficiency.

232
00:17:38.298 --> 00:17:41.706
A two-step transmitter instead
modulates at an intermediate

233
00:17:41.746 --> 00:17:45.602
frequency and then upconverts,
which makes filtering easier.

234
00:17:45.652 --> 00:17:49.375
This quadrature idea is
quadrature amplitude modulation,

235
00:17:49.415 --> 00:17:53.407
QAM: two carriers 90 degrees
apart, and with the right

236
00:17:53.447 --> 00:17:58.367
baseband signals it can generate
AM, FM or digital constellations.

237
00:18:00.017 --> 00:18:03.399
The workhorse receiver is
the superheterodyne: a mixer

238
00:18:03.439 --> 00:18:06.949
multiplies the incoming signal
by a local oscillator and

239
00:18:06.989 --> 00:18:11.268
produces the difference frequency,
the intermediate frequency.

240
00:18:11.318 --> 00:18:15.434
Tuning is done by moving the local
oscillator, while all the selective,

241
00:18:15.474 --> 00:18:19.239
high-gain filtering is done at a
fixed IF, which gives excellent

242
00:18:19.279 --> 00:18:24.254
sensitivity and selectivity; the price
is more circuitry and power consumption.

243
00:18:24.304 --> 00:18:28.232
Its weakness is the image: a
signal on the other side of the

244
00:18:28.272 --> 00:18:32.723
local oscillator, at the same
distance, also produces the IF.

245
00:18:32.773 --> 00:18:37.146
So the RF filter before the mixer must
reject the image frequency, which

246
00:18:37.186 --> 00:18:41.888
is why the slides list the problem of
the image among the design issues.

247
00:18:43.538 --> 00:18:46.825
In last year's exam the
receiver question was short.

248
00:18:46.875 --> 00:18:49.453
The architecture is the
superheterodyne, which

249
00:18:49.493 --> 00:18:54.737
converts every channel to the
same fixed IF of 455 kHz.

250
00:18:54.787 --> 00:18:58.207
With the local oscillator above
the signal, its frequency is

251
00:18:58.247 --> 00:19:05.749
5.5 plus 0.455, which is 5.955 MHz,
the answer in the solved copy.

252
00:19:05.799 --> 00:19:10.643
The image is then on the other
side of the oscillator, at 5.955

253
00:19:10.683 --> 00:19:17.213
plus 0.455, which is 6.41 MHz,
and the RF filter must remove it.

254
00:19:17.263 --> 00:19:23.115
One of the solved copies also gives
the low-side choice, 5.045 MHz, whose

255
00:19:23.155 --> 00:19:29.397
image would be at 4.59 MHz; either is
correct if you say which one you chose.

256
00:19:31.047 --> 00:19:36.082
To summarise part one: the wavelength is
c over f times the square root of the

257
00:19:36.122 --> 00:19:41.741
permittivity, and aviation uses bands from
kilohertz beacons to gigahertz radars.

258
00:19:41.791 --> 00:19:45.327
Low frequencies travel as
ground waves, VHF and above

259
00:19:45.367 --> 00:19:48.772
as space waves limited by
the radio horizon, with the

260
00:19:48.812 --> 00:19:53.972
1.23 rule in feet and nautical
miles, and HF as sky waves.

261
00:19:54.022 --> 00:19:58.444
For sky waves, the critical frequency
is 9 times the root of the electron

262
00:19:58.484 --> 00:20:04.647
density, the MUF is fc over the sine of
the elevation, and you work at 85 % of it.

263
00:20:04.697 --> 00:20:07.734
AM needs twice the highest
message frequency, FM

264
00:20:07.774 --> 00:20:11.119
follows Carson's rule, and
the superheterodyne converts

265
00:20:11.159 --> 00:20:15.264
everything to a fixed IF, at
the cost of an image frequency.

266
00:20:15.314 --> 00:20:19.148
In part two we turn to the
numbers that dominate the exam:

267
00:20:19.188 --> 00:20:24.348
decibels, noise figure, intercept
points, sensitivity and dynamic range.
